-- title:
--   Centre-unfolding identity for twisted orbital integrals on GL₂
-- statement:
--   Let $K \subseteq L$ be a Galois extension of number fields with $\sigma \in \mathrm{Gal}(L/K)$ such that every element of $\mathrm{Gal}(L/K)$ lies in the subgroup generated by $\sigma$, and let $D$ be an idelic Galois descent datum for $L/K$, i.e. a homomorphism from $\mathrm{Gal}(L/K)$ to the ring automorphisms of $\mathbb{A}_L$ that is continuous and compatible with $L \to \mathbb{A}_L$. The measure data are: Haar measures $\nu_{Z,L}$ on $\mathbb{A}_L^\times$, $\nu_K$ on $\mathbb{A}_K^\times$, $\mu_H$ (also right invariant) on the closed subgroup $H \le \mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{A}_L)$ of those $h$ whose $(1,0)$ and $(0,1)$ entries vanish and for which $\sigma(h)h^{-1}$ is central, $\mu$ on $\mathrm{GL}_2(L \otimes_K \mathbb{A}_K)$, $\mu_{A_K}$ on the closed subgroup $A_K \le \mathbb{A}_L^\times$ of units coming from $\mathbb{A}_K^\times$ along the base-change ring homomorphism, and $\mu_N$ on the closed subgroup $N^1$ of ideles of idelic norm $1$; together with positive constants $c_\mu, c_H, c_\tau, c_N$ and comparison hypotheses: integration against $\mu$ of a function pulled back along [`AutomorphicForm.baseChangeGL`](def/AutomorphicForm_BaseChangePlaces.html#L69) is $c_\mu$ times integration against `adelicGLHaar`; integration over $H$ is $c_H$ times the integral over $\mathbb{A}_L^\times \times \mathbb{A}_K^\times$ of the value at $\mathrm{diag}$-type elements `centralScalar z * baseChangeGL (toTensorGL (diagUnits2 a 1))`; integration over $A_K$ matches integration over $\mathbb{A}_K^\times$; and integration over $N^1$ is $c_N$ times the integral over the $A_K$-orbit space of $\mathbb{A}_L^\times$, with the `HaarQuotient` measure of $\nu_{Z,L}$, of $\sigma(q.\mathrm{out})\,q.\mathrm{out}^{-1}$ computed through `D.unitsAct`. Then, for every $t \in \mathrm{GL}_2(L)$ with vanishing off-diagonal entries and $N_{L/K}(t_{00}/t_{11}) \neq 1$, every $\delta \in \mathrm{GL}_2(L \otimes_K \mathbb{A}_K)$ whose base change is the image of $t$ under `globalPoints`, every Haar measure $\tau$ on the $\sigma$-twisted centralizer $\{s : s\delta\sigma(s)^{-1} = \delta\}$ satisfying the comparison that integration over it is $c_\tau$ times the integral over $\mathbb{A}_K^\times \times \mathbb{A}_K^\times$ of the value at `toTensorGL (diagUnits2 p.1 p.2)`, and every continuous compactly supported $\varphi : \mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{A}_L) \to \mathbb{C}$, two assertions hold for each idele $w$. First, if $I$ is a twisted orbital integral value (in the sense of `IsTwistedOrbitalIntegralOn`) of the pullback along `baseChangeGL` of $g \mapsto \varphi(\mathrm{diag}(w,w)\,g)$ at $\delta$ relative to $\mu$ and $\tau$, then $(c_N c_\tau/(c_H c_\mu)) \cdot I$ equals the integral over the $H$-orbit space of $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{A}_L)$, with the `HaarQuotient` measure of `adelicGLHaar`, of $\int_{N^1} \varphi\big(q.\mathrm{out}^{-1}\, t\, (\mathrm{diag}(nw,nw)\,\sigma(q.\mathrm{out}))\big)\,d\mu_N$, where $t$ is viewed in $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{A}_L)$ and $\sigma$ acts by `sigmaAdelicAct`. Second, the same holds with $I$ replaced by a weighted twisted orbital value $J$ for the weight $x \mapsto -\log \mathrm{ht}(\mathrm{bc}(x)) - \log \mathrm{ht}(w_2 \cdot \mathrm{bc}(x))$, with $\mathrm{ht}$ the adelic height on $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{A}_L)$ and $w_2$ the adelic Weyl element, the right-hand side acquiring the factor $-\log \mathrm{ht}(q.\mathrm{out}) - \log \mathrm{ht}(w_2\, q.\mathrm{out})$ inside the orbit-space integral.
--
--   This is the second step of the centre-unfolding comparison in the twisted trace formula for base change from $K$ to $L$ for $\mathrm{GL}_2$: it identifies a twisted orbital integral, and its logarithmic-height-weighted companion, at a regular diagonal element with an explicit double integral over $H \backslash \mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{A}_L)$ and the norm-one ideles, carrying the full normalising constant $c_N c_\tau/(c_H c_\mu)$. It feeds the subsequent reformulation of this identity over the quotient by the kernel of the idelic norm.
